@[expose] public section

/-!
# Bézout domains are coherent rings

Let `A` be a Bézout domain. This file proves that `A` is a coherent ring, that is, that every
finitely generated ideal of `A` is finitely presented as an `A`-module.

The argument is the source's. A finitely generated ideal `I` of a Bézout ring is principal
(`IsBezout.isPrincipal_of_FG`), say `I = A ∙ f`. Since `A` is a domain, multiplication by `f` is
an injective endomorphism of `A` whenever `f ≠ 0`, so it identifies `A` with `A ∙ f`; and for
`f = 0` the ideal is `⊥`. Either way `I` is free, hence projective, and it is finitely generated
by hypothesis, so it is finitely presented (`Module.finitePresentation_of_projective`). A
valuation ring is a local Bézout domain, so this contains the source's statement.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Coherent.BezoutDomain.solution`: the proof of Stacks 0EWV, as stated in
  `IsBezout.instIsCoherentRing`.

## Implementation notes

The freeness step is separated out as `Stacks.Coherent.BezoutDomain.free_span_singleton`, in the
generality it holds in: over a domain, the submodule generated by one element of a torsion-free
module is free. It is not in Mathlib, where its finite-dimensional shadow is
`FiniteDimensional.span_singleton`; `Module.Free.span_singleton` would be its name there. Freeness
rather than projectivity is what the one-element span gives directly, and projectivity is then
instance search. Its two cases are the two Mathlib facts about a one-element span over a domain:
`Submodule.span_zero_singleton` and `LinearEquiv.toSpanNonzeroSingleton`, the isomorphism `R ≃ₗ[R] R
∙ x` for `x ≠ 0`.

## References

* [Stacks 0EWV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EWV)
* [Stacks 090Q](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/090Q)
-/

namespace Stacks.Coherent.BezoutDomain

/-- Over a domain, the submodule of a torsion-free module generated by a single element `x` is
free: it is `⊥` when `x = 0`, and multiplication by `x` identifies it with the ring otherwise. -/
private theorem free_span_singleton {R M : Type*} [Ring R] [IsDomain R] [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module R M] [Module.IsTorsionFree R M] (x : M) : Module.Free R ↥(R ∙ x) := by
  rcases eq_or_ne x 0 with rfl | hx
  · rw [Submodule.span_zero_singleton]
    infer_instance
  · exact Module.Free.of_equiv (LinearEquiv.toSpanNonzeroSingleton R M x hx)

public theorem solution (A : Type*) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsBezout A] :
    IsCoherentRing A where
  isCoherent.finite := inferInstance
  isCoherent.fg_submodule_finitePresentation I hI := by
    obtain ⟨f, hf⟩ := IsBezout.isPrincipal_of_FG I hI
    have : Module.Finite A ↥I := Module.Finite.iff_fg.mpr hI
    have : Module.Free A ↥I := hf ▸ free_span_singleton f
    exact Module.finitePresentation_of_projective A ↥I

end Stacks.Coherent.BezoutDomain

/-!
# Bézout domains are coherent rings

A Bézout domain `A` is a coherent ring: `A` is finitely generated over itself, and every
finitely generated ideal of `A` is finitely presented as an `A`-module. Indeed a finitely
generated ideal `I` of a Bézout ring is principal, say `I = (f)`; if `f = 0` then `I` is the zero
module, and otherwise `f` is a nonzerodivisor because `A` is a domain, so multiplication by `f`
is an isomorphism `A ≃ₗ[A] ↥I`. Either way `I` is free of finite rank, hence finitely presented.

In particular a valuation ring is coherent, which is the form the source states: a valuation ring
is exactly a local Bézout domain, and its finitely generated ideals are principal. Coherence is
the hypothesis under which products of flat modules are flat, so this is the reason a valuation
ring behaves like a Noetherian ring for the purposes of finite presentation even when it is not
Noetherian.

## Main results

* `IsBezout.instIsCoherentRing`: a Bézout domain is a coherent ring; a valuation ring is one
  through `ValuationRing.instIsBezout`.

## Implementation notes

The source (Stacks 0EWV) states this for a valuation ring, but its proof uses only that finitely
generated ideals are principal and that `A` is a domain, never that `A` is local. Since
`ValuationRing.iff_local_bezout_domain` reads `ValuationRing A ↔ IsLocalRing A ∧ IsBezout A`,
locality would be a hypothesis the conclusion does not need, so the statement is made for a
Bézout domain. The valuation ring case is then found by instance search through
`ValuationRing.instIsBezout` — the very lemma the source's proof cites (Stacks 090Q) — and is not
given a name of its own, being a composite with no extra content. The gain is genuine: the ring
of all algebraic integers and the ring of entire functions on `ℂ` are non-Noetherian Bézout
domains that are not valuation rings, and `ℤ` is a Bézout domain that is not one either.

The domain hypothesis is not dropped, and cannot be: over a Bézout ring the ideal `(f)` is
`A / ann f`, which is finitely presented only when `ann f` is finitely generated, and a domain is
what makes `ann f` trivial for `f ≠ 0`.

The conclusion is stated as an instance rather than as an implication, since `IsCoherentRing` is
(reducibly) the class `Module.Coherent A A` and the source uses this example to hand coherence to
later arguments about valuation rings; the name mutates that of the sibling
`ValuationRing.instIsBezout` in the same way.

Faithfulness. (1) Every hypothesis is used by the conclusion: `IsBezout A` makes a finitely
generated ideal principal and `IsDomain A` makes a nonzero principal ideal free, which are the
source's two steps; locality is absent precisely because the proof does not use it.
(2) The scalars and the base are the source's: coherence is coherence of `A` as a module over
itself, the submodules at issue are the ideals of `A`, and finite presentation is over `A`, with
no localization, completion or base change. (3) The source names no canonical map; what it does
name — finitely generated ideal, principal, free, finitely presented — is pinned through
`IsCoherentRing`, whose content over `A` is `Module.FinitePresentation A ↥I` for every finitely
generated ideal `I`, rather than restated as the existence of generators and relations.
(4) The instantiation is not degenerate: the ring of all algebraic integers is a witness that is
neither Noetherian nor a valuation ring, and the source's own case is witnessed by the valuation
ring of the field of Puiseux series over a field, whose value group is `ℚ`.

## References

* [Stacks 0EWV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EWV)
* [Stacks 090Q](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/090Q), the Bézout property of a valuation
  ring, which is `ValuationRing.instIsBezout`
-/

@[expose] public section

/-- A Bézout domain is a coherent ring: a finitely generated ideal is principal
(`IsBezout.isPrincipal_of_FG`), hence free since the ring is a domain, hence finitely presented.
The source states this for a valuation ring, which is a local Bézout domain
(`ValuationRing.iff_local_bezout_domain`); locality is not used. -/
@[stacks 0EWV "Stated for a Bézout domain: the source's proof of the valuation ring case uses \
only that finitely generated ideals are principal and that the ring is a domain. The valuation \
ring case is instance search through `ValuationRing.instIsBezout`."]
public instance IsBezout.instIsCoherentRing (A : Type*) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsBezout A] :
    IsCoherentRing A :=
      by apply Stacks.Coherent.BezoutDomain.solution

end
